! REPRODUCER for a disagreement between the model and the Reference
! Manual's text, found 2026-11-15 by C22-6578 Problem 3.8 comment (b)
! (decks/problem_3_8_no_eplus.strap; docs/NEXT.md, "C22-6578 CHAPTER 3"
! item 5): a single-precision normalized ADD whose 96-bit intermediate
! sum has nothing in its high-order 48 bits.
!
! Reference Manual printed p.87 (PDF 90), read in the ink, of ADD:
!   "When normalized operation is specified, the entire 96-bit
!   intermediate sum fraction and overflow bit are shifted to form a
!   normalized fraction and the result exponent is adjusted
!   accordingly.  This normalization is not performed if the overflow
!   bit and high-order 48 bits of the intermediate sum are zero."
!   "Lost Significance (LS).  This indicator is turned on in addition
!   when at least one operand fraction is non-zero if the high-order 48
!   bits of the intermediate sum and overflow bit are zero and the
!   intermediate result exponent flag is 0.  In this case, the final
!   sum fraction is zero and a normal order of magnitude zero is
!   produced with no further normalization, if specified, or exponent
!   change."
! and printed p.82, "Zero Definition": "In single-precision operations,
! the high-order 48 bits and the overflow bit of the 96-bit intermediate
! result fraction are tested for zero ... if the tested bits are zero,
! post-normalization, if specified, is suspended.  The intermediate
! result exponent field, the zero fraction, and fraction sign become
! the final result value."
!
! The model's fpAdd (bsv/StretchFP.bsv) tests all 97 bits of the sum for
! zero and otherwise normalizes the whole of it, so a sum that survives
! only in the low-order half is shifted back up and returned.
!
! SMALL is 3 x 2**-60 = .11 x 2**-58.  Each case adds it to a ZERO
! FRACTION with a larger exponent, which shifts SMALL's fraction right
! by the exponent difference (printed p.86):
!
!   CASE 1.  + a word of zero bits (exponent 0): difference 58.  The
!     two fraction bits land in places 59 and 60 of the 96.  High-order
!     48 zero: the result is the zero fraction with exponent 0.
!         R1 expected 0000000000000000
!   CASE 2.  + a zero fraction with exponent -10: difference 48, the
!     boundary.  The bits land in places 49 and 50, the first two of
!     the low-order half.  High-order 48 zero.
!         R2 expected 0150000000000000  (exponent -10, zero fraction)
!   CASE 3, the control.  + a zero fraction with exponent -11:
!     difference 47.  The leading bit lands in place 48, the last of
!     the high-order half, so the sum is normalized as a whole and
!     comes back as SMALL.
!         R3 expected 075C000000000000  (.11 x 2**-58)
!
! OBSERVED ON THE MODEL 2026-11-15, ASM=strap2, PC=73 (reached):
!     R1 = 075C000000000000   FAIL  (SMALL came back)
!     R2 = 075C000000000000   FAIL  (SMALL came back)
!     R3 = 075C000000000000   PASS
!   and the indicator word was 0000001000000120: PSH (bit 27) on, LS
!   (bit 26) off.
!
! WHICH IS RIGHT IS NOT SETTLED HERE.  The manual's text is quoted
! above.  Against it: C22-6578 printed p.32, Problem 3.8 comment (b),
! says a program that adds numbers of exponent -48 and less to a word
! of zero bits suffers no "damage", which is so only if the machine
! behaves as the model does -- though comment (a) of the same problem
! describes the manual's behaviour.  jb_af1c's addition sections check
! PSH for far-apart exponents (decks/fp_arith_f204d.strap, F204D7) but
! the deck headers do not say they check the SUM in this case; that
! listing is where a third witness would be.

START   L(U), SMALL
        +(N), ZERO
        ST(U), R1
        L(U), SMALL
        +(N), ZM10
        ST(U), R2
        L(U), SMALL
        +(N), ZM11
        ST(U), R3
        BEW, $
SMALL   DD(N), 3X-58
ZERO    DD(N), 0
ZM10    DD(N), 0.0X-10
ZM11    DD(N), 0.0X-11
R1      DR(N), (1)
R2      DR(N), (1)
R3      DR(N), (1)
        END, START
